Installation Instructions for Twist
=====================================

Copyright (C) 2026 Chris

This file contains generic installation instructions.  For specific
instructions for a particular system, see the README file.

   Briefly, the shell commands `./configure; make; make install' should
configure, compile, and install this package.

   The `configure' shell script attempts to guess correct values for
various system-dependent variables used during compilation.  It uses
the values it finds, or the defaults if it cannot find a suitable value,
to create a `Makefile' in each directory of the package.

   The `Makefile' contains rules for building the package.  To build
the package, you need to run `make'.  This will create the executables,
libraries, and so on that the package provides.

   Finally, `make install' will install the built files into the
installation directories that were chosen during configuration.

   By default, `make install' will install the package's files in
`/usr/local/bin', `/usr/local/man', etc.  You can specify an
installation prefix other than `/usr/local' by giving `configure' the
option `--prefix=PATH'.

Compilers and Options
---------------------

   Some systems require unusual options for compilation or linking that
the `configure' script does not know about.  Run `./configure --help'
for information on some of the options that `configure' accepts.

   For some systems, you may need to set environment variables to specify
libraries or include files.  To do this, you can use the following
variables when running `configure':

     CPPFLAGS   C preprocessor flags, e.g. `-I<include dir>' if you have
                 headers in a non-standard directory `<include dir>'.
     LIBS       library flags, e.g. `-L<lib dir>' if you have libraries
                 in a non-standard directory `<lib dir>'.

   You can also set the CC and CXX environment variables to specify
a particular C or C++ compiler to use.

Installation Names
------------------

   By default, `make install' will install the package's commands under
`/usr/local/bin', data files under `/usr/local/share', etc.  You can
specify an installation prefix other than `/usr/local' by giving
`configure' the option `--prefix=PATH'.

   You can specify separate installation prefixes for
architecture-specific files and architecture-independent files.  If you
pass the option `--exec-prefix=PATH' to `configure', the package will
use PATH as the prefix for installing programs and libraries.
Documentation and other data files will still use the regular prefix.

   In addition, if you use an unusual directory layout you can specify
the installation directories for each type of file by giving options to
configure.  For example:

     ./configure --prefix=/usr/local --exec-prefix=/usr/local 
                 --bindir=/usr/local/bin --sbindir=/usr/local/sbin 
                 --libexecdir=/usr/local/libexec 
                 --datadir=/usr/local/share --sysconfdir=/etc
                 --sharedstatedir=/usr/local/com --localstatedir=/var
                 --libdir=/usr/local/lib --includedir=/usr/local/include
                 --oldincludedir=/usr/include --infodir=/usr/local/info
                 --mandir=/usr/local/man

Optional Features
-----------------

   Some packages pay attention to `--enable-FEATURE' options that they
get from `configure', where FEATURE indicates an optional part of the
package.  For example, `--enable-debug' might enable debugging output
for a program.  You can use `--disable-FEATURE' to disable such a
feature.

   Run `./configure --help' to see all available options.

Specifying the System Type
--------------------------

   There may be some features that `configure' cannot figure out
automatically, but that can be specified by the user.  You can specify
the system type by giving `configure' the option `--build=TYPE', where
TYPE can be either a short system type like `sun4' or a canonical
system type like `sparc-sun-sunos4.1.3'.

Sharing Defaults
----------------

   If you want to set default values for `configure' scripts you run in
the future, you can create a site shell script called `config.site'
that gives default values for variables like `CC', `cppflags', etc.
This is useful for setting system-specific variables that you want to
use for all packages you compile.  `configure' looks for `PREFIX/share/config.site'
if it exists, then `PREFIX/etc/config.site' if it exists, where PREFIX
is the installation prefix you specified or the default `/usr/local'.

Operating System Specifics
--------------------------

   On some systems, you need to specify the machine type to configure.
For example, on a SparcStation, you can use:

     ./configure sparc-sun-sunos4.1.3

   However, `configure' can usually determine the machine type itself.

   On systems that have a case-insensitive or case-tracking file system,
such as Windows 95, the installation directory should be specified in
lowercase.  For example:

     ./configure --prefix=c:/gnu

   Some packages require a different version of the GNU C library than
the one found by `configure'.  In that case, you can specify the
library version explicitly.  For example:

     ./configure --with-gnu-ld=ld

   On some systems, the assembler needs special flags.  You can specify
them with the `ASFLAGS' environment variable.  For example:

     ASFLAGS='-traditional-cpp' ./configure

Installation
------------

   To install the package:

     1. Run `configure' with appropriate options to generate a Makefile.

     2. Type `make' to compile the package.

     3. Optionally, type `make check' to run any self-tests that come
        with the package.

     4. Type `make install' to install the programs and any data files
        and documentation.

     5. Optionally, type `make installcheck' to verify the installation.
        This is not required, but it can catch installation problems.

     6. Optionally, type `make clean' to remove the program binaries
        and object files from the source directory.

   The commands `make clean' and `make distclean' can be used to remove
files that were created by `make'.  `make clean' removes the files
from the build that are created by the compilation process, while
`make distclean' also removes the files that `configure' created.

   If the package supports it, `make uninstall' can be used to remove the
files that were installed by `make install'.

   The files `configure' and `Makefile.in' are designed to be used with
a Bourne-compatible shell.  If your `/bin/sh' is buggy, try using bash
or ksh instead.

Compilers
---------

   The GNU build system requires a C compiler that conforms to the ISO
C89 standard.  If your system does not have such a compiler, you can
install the GNU C compiler (gcc) from <https://gcc.gnu.org/>.

   If you are using a C++ compiler, it must support the ISO C++98
standard.  If your compiler does not support this standard, you can
install g++ from <https://gcc.gnu.org/>.

Troubleshooting
---------------

   If you encounter problems during the build or installation, first
check the README file for any system-specific instructions.

   If the build fails, check the config.log file for error messages.
This file contains the output of the configure script and can help
diagnose problems.

   If you need help, you can send a bug report to <chris@localhost>.
Please include the following information in your report:

     * The version of the package you are trying to install.
     * The system type (output of `uname -a').
     * The output of `config.guess' (if available).
     * The exact error message you received.
     * The contents of config.log.

Reporting Bugs
--------------

   Report bugs to <chris@localhost>.

   Please include the version number of the package, the system type,
and a complete description of the problem in your bug report.
